# Clean Code Standards

## Error Handling
- Never use bare `except:` — catch specific exceptions
- Collectors: catch per-item so one failure doesn't skip the rest
- UI pages: use `st.error()` for user-facing errors
- Always close sessions even on error:

```python
session = get_session()
try:
    results = session.query(Company).all()
finally:
    session.close()
```

When a function accepts an optional `session`, only close what it created:

```python
def do_work(session=None) -> Result:
    own_session = session is None
    if own_session:
        session = get_session()
    try:
        # ... work ...
    finally:
        if own_session:
            session.close()
```

## Data Flow
- Config values come from `config.py` (env vars with defaults), never hardcoded
- New constants go in `config.py` (types, feeds) or `scoring/weights.py` (score params)
- Pass `session` explicitly rather than creating sessions deep in call stacks

## Module Headers
Every major module (`config.py`, `database/models.py`, `database/db.py`, `scoring/engine.py`, `scoring/weights.py`, `ai/embeddings.py`, `ai/summarizer.py`, `ai/signal_extractor.py`, `ai/brief_generator.py`, `collectors/base.py`, `collectors/runner.py`) must start with a 3-line comment header:

```python
# Why: <8 words max>
# Deps: <8 words max>
# How: <8 words max>
```

Example:

```python
# Why: Scores prospects from collected signal data
# Deps: SQLAlchemy models, weights config, datetime
# How: Recency-decayed weighted sum per signal type
```

Page files (`pages/*.py`) and test files are excluded.

## Type Hints
- Add type hints on function signatures (params and return types)
- Use `X | None` syntax over `Optional[X]`
